>> title: Tonkotsu Broth
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, japanese, stock, ramen, stovetop
>> counters: Ramen Shop
>> aka: tonkotsu, tonkotsu broth, 豚骨, とんこつ, pork bone broth, white pork broth, Hakata broth, ramen broth, milky pork broth
>> pairs-with: tonkotsu-ramen
>> servings: 8
>> time: 9 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the difference between white broth and grey broth is mechanical: eight hours at a hard rolling boil beats the collagen and fat into an emulsion, and letting it drop to a simmer lets them separate and sit there. Take it off entirely and start cold rather than nurse it back
>> step.1: A shop boils this 12 to 18 hours and tops the pot up as it goes; this is the one-off home version — 8 hours, one pot, and genuinely white.
>> step.2: parboil 30 min, then scrub every bone
>> step.3: boil hard 8 hr, topping the water up
>> step.4: stir the aromatics in, last hour
>> step.5: strain, pressing the softened marrow through

A shop boils this twelve to eighteen hours and never empties the pot — it tops up, draws off, and starts again. A home pot cannot run that loop. What follows is the one-off version: eight hours, one pot, and it is genuinely white at the end of it.

Cover @pork trotters{2%lb}(900 g; split lengthways by the butcher), @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) and @pork fatback{6%oz}(170 g) with @cold water{5%qt}(4.7 L) in a #stockpot{}, bring to a hard boil and ~parboil{30%min}, then tip the lot into the sink and scrub every bone under the tap until nothing brown or grey clings to it. Scrub the pot too. This is the only chance to take the scum out; after this the boil drives it back into the broth instead of lifting it to the top.

Return @&(~1)scrubbed bones{} to the pot with @water{6%qt}(5.7 L) and ~boil{8%hr} at a hard rolling boil, topping the water back to its starting line every hour or so. Never let it fall to a simmer. The whole difference between white broth and grey broth is mechanical: a rolling boil beats the collagen and the fat into the water as an emulsion, and a simmer lets them separate and sit there. If you have to leave it, take it off the heat entirely and start again cold.

Drop @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @garlic{1%head}(halved across), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced) and @scallion greens{4%stalks} into @&(~1)broth{} for the last hour only, ~boil{1%hr}. In from the start they boil to nothing and leave a vegetal sweetness that reads as thin.

Strain @&(~1)broth{} through a coarse sieve, pressing the softened marrow and knuckle cartilage through with a ladle — that pulp is body, and leaving it in the sieve is leaving the broth behind. It should coat a spoon hot and set to a firm jelly cold.
